<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> Why is film's subject matter described as complex?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Because film combines the composition of painting, the narrative of literature, the performance of theater, and the rhythm of music.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Because it uses no sound.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">Because it is limited to one subject.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Because it is always about politics.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The camera adds its own dimensions: movement through space, compression or expansion of time, and the close-up.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Film draws on several art forms at once. Look for the answer that names how many it combines.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> Which shot fills the screen with a face or object?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">A close-up.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">A crane shot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">An establishing shot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">A long shot.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A close-up places you inside a feeling, while a long shot places a person in a world.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">An establishing shot sets the scene from far away, and a long shot shows distant figures. The shot that fills the screen is a close-up.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> What is the difference between a continuity cut and a jump cut?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">A continuity cut is longer.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">They are the same.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">A continuity cut uses sound; a jump cut does not.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">A continuity cut is a smooth transition that preserves the sense of time flowing; a jump cut is abrupt and disorienting.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Both are tools of editing, the assembly of shots.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Continuity is about smoothness. A jump cut deliberately breaks it.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Cross-cutting is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">a cut between a close-up and itself.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">alternating between two simultaneous events.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">a very long single shot.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">a transition that fades to black.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Cross-cutting makes two events at once feel connected, as in the Odessa Steps when we cut between soldiers and fleeing crowds.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Cross-cutting alternates between two actions happening at the same time.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Montage is</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">an actor's performance.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">a sequence of images that creates meaning through juxtaposition instead of narrative continuity.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">a film's music.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">a long shot of a landscape.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. In montage, the meaning comes from the editing: the rhythm of the cuts and the accumulation of visual shock.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Montage creates meaning by placing images side by side. The meaning is in the juxtaposition.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> What does the concept of the auteur claim?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">That the audience writes the film.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">That the screenwriter alone creates a film.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">That the director is the true author of a film, the person whose vision unifies all the elements.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">That a film has no author.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The idea came from French film criticism in the 1950s and remains influential.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Auteur is French for author. The claim concerns the director's unifying vision.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> In the Odessa Steps sequence, where does the meaning come from?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">From a single long take.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">From the opening credits.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">From the dialogue.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">From the editing: the juxtaposition of images, the rhythm of the cuts, and the accumulation of visual shock.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. No single shot tells the story. Eisenstein's montage creates chaos, terror, and injustice.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The sequence is famous because the editing, not any one shot, makes the effect.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> What visual style did Orson Welles use in the opening of Citizen Kane?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Silent film conventions.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">Deep focus, unusual camera angles, and long takes, with dissolves moving closer to Xanadu while one lit window stays in place.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Animated drawings.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">Only close-ups of actors.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Deep focus keeps foreground and background sharp. Welles was 25 when he directed the film.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Welles's visual style used deep focus and long takes. The opening uses dissolves toward a mansion.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> In Bong Joon-ho's Parasite, why is architecture described as content and not just setting?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">Because the film is about building houses.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Because the film has no characters.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">The vertical geography of the film, a modernist house on a hill and a semi-basement below, embodies its subject matter: economic inequality.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Because the houses are cartoons.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Bong designed the Park house for the film, so architecture functions as artistic form within another art form.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The lesson says the houses' positions up and down the hill are inseparable from the film's subject, inequality.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> What did Georges Méliès add to early cinema in A Trip to the Moon?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">Special effects and storytelling made with painted sets and tricks such as stopping the camera, changing something in the scene, and starting again.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Documentary footage of an actual trip.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Synchronized sound and color film.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">The first close-ups of faces.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. As a stage magician, Méliès saw that the camera could create illusions as well as record.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Méliès was a magician. His film uses staged sets and camera tricks that make things appear and vanish.</div>
  </div>
</div>

